{"name":"Cherokee","hex":"#f5cd82","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Cherokee","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A member of an indigenous North American people."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["An indigenous North American people.","Their Iroquoian language, spoken in Oklahoma and North Carolina."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Cherokee"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cherokee","blocks":[{"text":"The Cherokee or Tsalagi people are one of the Indigenous peoples of the Southeastern Woodlands of the United States. Prior to the 18th century, they were concentrated in their ancestral homelands, living in towns along river valleys in what is now southwestern North Carolina, southeastern Tennessee, southwestern Virginia, parts of western South Carolina, northern Georgia, and northeastern Alabama, with hunting grounds extending into Kentucky. Together, these lands encompassed approximately 40,000 square miles."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Henry_Inman_-_Sequoyah_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/330px-Henry_Inman_-_Sequoyah_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cherokee","wikidata":"Q105405"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mound_Builder_City.jpeg/500px-Mound_Builder_City.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Mound_Builder_City.jpeg/500px-Mound_Builder_City.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMound_Builder_City.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg/500px-Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg/1280px-Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg/500px-Smoky_Mtn_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Great Smoky Mountains","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASmoky_Mtn_View.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg/500px-Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg/1280px-Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg/500px-Indians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An annotated copy of a hand-painted Catawba deerskin map of the tribes between Charleston (left) and Virginia (right) following the displacements of a century of disease and slavery among the indigenous peoples of the Americas and the 1715–7 Yamasee War. The Cherokee are labelled as \"Cherrikies\".","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndians_NW_of_South_Carolina.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Three_Cherokee.jpg/500px-Three_Cherokee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Three_Cherokee.jpg/500px-Three_Cherokee.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"After the Anglo-Cherokee War, bitterness remained between the two groups. In 1765, Henry Timberlake took three Cherokee chiefs to London meet the Crown and help strengthen the newly declared peace.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThree_Cherokee.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Mather_Brown_-_Portrait_of_Major_John_Norton_as_Mohawk_Chief_Teyoninhokarawen_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Mather_Brown_-_Portrait_of_Major_John_Norton_as_Mohawk_Chief_Teyoninhokarawen_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Mather_Brown_-_Portrait_of_Major_John_Norton_as_Mohawk_Chief_Teyoninhokarawen_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/1280px-Mather_Brown_-_Portrait_of_Major_John_Norton_as_Mohawk_Chief_Teyoninhokarawen_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Mather_Brown_-_Portrait_of_Major_John_Norton_as_Mohawk_Chief_Teyoninhokarawen_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg/500px-Mather_Brown_-_Portrait_of_Major_John_Norton_as_Mohawk_Chief_Teyoninhokarawen_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portrait of Major John Norton (Mohawk chief) as Mohawk Chief Teyoninhokarawen by Mather Brown, ca. 1805. Yale Center for British Art (his father was a Cherokee while John Norton adopted by the Mohawks)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMather_Brown_-_Portrait_of_Major_John_Norton_as_Mohawk_Chief_Teyoninhokarawen_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Major_ridge.jpg/500px-Major_ridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Major_ridge.jpg/1280px-Major_ridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Major_ridge.jpg/500px-Major_ridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Portrait of Major Ridge